    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah here is the role policy. i'm also kinda worried about why it's being so stingy with permissions. it feels like there's some weird blockage preventing the access.\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Principal\": {\n        \"Service\": \"lambda.amazonaws.com\"\n      },\n      \"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\ndoes this look right for the trust relationship? i'm still getting that access denied error when the code runs.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your trust relationship looks almost correct, but the order is different. Typically, the principal attribute should specify who's being trusted (in this case, AWS Lambda) without an effect statement. Try rearranging it to:\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Principal\": {\n        \"Service\": \"lambda.amazonaws.com\"\n      },\n      \"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Principal\": {\n        \"Service\": \"lambda.amazonaws.com\"\n      },\n      \"Action\": [\"s3:PutObject\", \"s3:PutObjectAcl\", \"s3:GetObject\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nThis states that AWS Lambda services can assume the role, which is necessary for running the Lambda function, and grants it the permissions to log to S3.",
      "source": "target",
